<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ephesus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayasoluk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ephesus was an ancient Greek city on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near modern Selçuk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, famed for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a>, one of the Seven Wonders of the World. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it was the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. As its harbour silted up, life moved to the nearby hill of Ayasoluk, which the Aydinids took in the early 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ephesus.jpg" alt="Ephesus" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ephesus_Celsus_Library_Fa%C3%A7ade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ephesus was an ancient Greek city on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, in present-day Selçuk in İzmir Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. People lived in the surrounding area by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, about 6000 BCE, and Hittite sources name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apasa">Apasa</a> as the capital of the kingdom of Arzawa, which some scholars identify with the later Ephesus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was founded as an Attic and Ionian colony in the 10th century BCE on Ayasuluk Hill. Legend names its founder as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/androclus">Androklos</a>, a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> who left home after the death of his father King Kodros, and who joined the twelve cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> together in the Ionian League. Ephesus took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> against Persian rule, in the Battle of Ephesus of 498 BCE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Granicus</a> in 334 BCE he was greeted warmly in Ephesus. The city passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> in 290 BCE and, as part of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 129 BCE.</p><p>Ephesus was a major centre of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The apostle Paul lived there for three years from 52 to 54 CE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> tells of a mob raised by the silversmith Demetrius, who feared for the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>. Ephesus remained the most important Byzantine city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 5th and 6th centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> built the basilica of St John. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> destroyed the city in 263, and the harbour was slowly silted up by the Küçükmenderes River, so that the city declined as a port.</p><p>In 1304 the town surrendered to a Turkish warlord, Sasa Bey, and was then ceded to the Aydinid principality, which stationed a navy in the harbour of Ayasuluğ. Ephesus was abandoned by the 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a>, completed around 550 BCE, once had over 100 marble pillars, and only one column stands. The Library of Celsus was built about 125 and held nearly 12,000 scrolls, and the Great Theatre may be the largest of the ancient world. Excavation began in 1863 under John Turtle Wood, and the Austrian Archaeological Institute, founded in 1898, still leads it. The ruins were designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ephesus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 569 John of <b>Ephesus</b> mentioned it for the first time, and a late 6th-century Greek document from Byzantine Egypt recording the sale of an Alodian slave girl confirms its existence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ephesus</b> lay on the west coast of Asia Minor near the mouth of the Cayster River, on a bay that has since silted up and left its ruins several kilometres inland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Croesus attacked <b>Ephesus</b> and brought all the Greek cities of the coast under tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lydia under Gyges fought them off around c. 665–660 BC, but in 644 BC the Cimmerians and the Thracian Treres took Sardis, killed Gyges and went on to destroy Magnesia on the Meander and the Artemision of <b>Ephesus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tyrian annals that the Jewish historian Josephus quoted from Menander of <b>Ephesus</b> say that he enlarged the island city and rebuilt its temples.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars at the libraries of Alexandria and Pergamum studied and edited the poems, among them Zenodotus of <b>Ephesus</b>, Aristophanes of Byzantium and Aristarchus of Samothrace.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
